INT. DEEP SPACE RELAY STATION - CONTROL ROOM - NIGHT CYCLE

Brass-toned consoles, curved glass, a chart table out of some earlier century's idea of the future. Soft music plays from a hidden speaker. A blue star turns slowly beyond the viewport.

COMMANDER EVELYN ADEYEMI, 50s, in a crisp gray uniform, stands at a console with a cup of tea. Beside her, the station's navigator LIEUTENANT PIERRE DUMONT, 30s, leans on the chart table over a spread of star maps. At the communications desk, the young radio operator ENSIGN TAMSIN BELL, 20s, wears a headset and absentmindedly twirls a pencil.

A soft chime. Tamsin sits up.

TAMSIN
Commander. Incoming signal on the long channel.

EVELYN
Source?

TAMSIN
Unknown. Wide beam, no authentication.

PIERRE
Probably a drifter.

EVELYN
Probably.

Tamsin flips a switch. A hush of static fills the room, and out of it a voice, warm, old, faintly amused, as if from the bottom of a well.

VOICE (V.O.)
(over speaker)
Relay Station Nine, this is a friend. Please do not be alarmed.

Pierre and Tamsin exchange a look. Evelyn sets her tea down.

EVELYN
Identify yourself.

VOICE (V.O.)
Names are a luxury. Call me the Traveler.

PIERRE
(whispering)
That's not a ship's name.

TRAVELER (V.O.)
I am not a ship.

The lights dim. A shape begins to resolve in the viewport, a tall, indistinct column of pale light rising out of the star's glare. Tamsin takes off her headset.

EVELYN
Ensign. Raise the shields.

TAMSIN
They're up, Commander. They've been up.

TRAVELER (V.O.)
There is no need. I am here only to deliver a warning.

The pale shape leans closer to the glass. Pierre steps back and knocks a star map to the floor. Evelyn does not blink.

EVELYN
What warning?

The shape raises what might be a hand. In the viewport, the blue star flickers once, twice.

TRAVELER (V.O.)
Your sun is not what you think.

Silence. The music, absurdly, continues. Evelyn crosses to the speaker, finds the knob, and turns it off.

EVELYN
Go on.

The Traveler's voice, when it comes, is very gentle.

TRAVELER (V.O.)
It is a door. And somebody is knocking.

Evelyn looks at Pierre and Tamsin. Neither speaks. Beyond the glass, the blue star flickers a third time, and the whole station groans like a ship in a swell.

TAMSIN
(small voice)
Commander, I think we should go home.

EVELYN
(quietly)
We are home.

The shape in the window lowers its hand, and the star goes dark.
